  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a theoretical framework applied to qualitative and historical trends, without providing specific quantitative metrics or statistical datasets to measure the precise scale of productivity declines or quality issues.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the neoliberal transformation of the Swedish construction sector using a mixed-methods approach grounded in historical materialism. It introduces a 'triaxial threat space' framework to map the interactions between housing commodification, production fragmentation, and the labor theory of value, tracing how these forces affect the industry's traditional high-road model.",
      "why": "The research demonstrates that shifting from community-focused building to market-driven commodity production directly causes labor deskilling, worker exploitation, declining productivity, and degraded construction quality. It establishes a structural link between macroeconomic political shifts and the physical degradation of both building standards and labor conditions."
    }
